  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 259 PM EDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nLatest GOES water vapor and RAP analysis reveal northwest flow aloft \nacross the Upper Great Lakes between persistent deep troughing over \nNE Canada and low amplitude ridging over the western half of the \nCONUS. This yields a pleasant summer afternoon across the UP with \nmostly sunny skies and highs generally in the upper 70s to low 80s. \nAn embedded disturbance over northern Ontario could bring a few \nshowers near Isle Royale through this evening, but otherwise expect \nmostly clear/sunny skies to prevail across the region tonight into \nFriday as surface high pressure lingers to the south across Lower \nMichigan. Thus expect similarly pleasant conditions on Friday as 850 \nmb temps inch up towards 15C and allow highs to warm a couple more \ndegrees into the low to mid 80s for Friday afternoon.\n\nThe next chance for precipitation arrives on Saturday as a stronger \nlow pressure system moves across northern Ontario and drags a \nsurface cold front across the UP Saturday morning. Severe potential \nappears limited by timing as the best forcing will have departed to \nthe east prior to peak heating, but will nonetheless have to monitor \nthe potential for a few strong to severe storms as guidance depicts \nbulk shear values increasing to 35-40 kt with MUCAPE of 500-1000 \nJ/kg through early Saturday. Thunderstorm chances will linger into \nthe afternoon as shear remains adequate and warm and humid \nconditions allow the air mass to recover behind the front, but \nstorms will be more hit or miss in nature given the lack of obvious \nforcing mechanisms. Precipitation amounts do not look overly \nimpressive/impactful as the NBM depicts around a 40% chance to \nreceive a wetting rain of a quarter inch or more across the northern \ntier of counties along Lake Superior, with closer to a 15-20% chance \nnear the WI border and Lake Michigan. Would expect precipitation to \nbe highly variable as locations that receive thunderstorms could \nsee much higher amounts given PWATS of 1.25-1.50\" on Saturday.\n\nRain and thunderstorm chances persist into Sunday and Monday as \nanother system moving over the top of the upstream ridge approaches \nfrom the west, but uncertainty over the track of this system results \nin relatively low forecast confidence at this time. Chances on \nSunday will revolve around the placement of a surface warm front \nahead of the low, as expected high temperatures well into the 80s \nshould yield plenty of instability to work with if the other \nelements come together. The next round of shower and thunderstorm \npotential will then come on Monday as the system tracks through the \nregion. SPC is keeping the best severe probs south of the UP for \nboth Sunday and Monday as of this writing, but will need to continue \nto pay close attention to the forecast as this will be highly \ndependent on the ultimate track and evolution of the system. \nOtherwise, the rest of next week looks fairly benign as the large \nscale pattern continues to favor a northwest flow regime with \ntemperatures close to seasonal norms and periodic precipitation \nchances as embedded systems move through the region.",
